# 23. Why this becomes extremely cheap

The architecture shifts cost from:

[
C \propto \text{number of reads}
]

toward:

[
C \propto \text{number of meaningful changes}
]

Suppose one scholarly synthesis costs enormous compute to create.

You pay once:

```text
research
LLMs
review
compilation
```

Then one million people/agents can read:

```text
the same immutable bytes
```

mostly through CDN caches.

This is basically perfect for scholarship because scholarly content changes comparatively slowly but can be read indefinitely.

The performance doctrine phrases it extremely well:

> scholarly work happens asynchronously once; reading it should be nearly free forever.

---

# 24. Why it becomes lightning fast for humans

Because:

```text
no DB
no SSR
no API waterfall
no giant React hydration
no graph reconstruction
```

for normal reading.

The hot path is:

```text
URL
↓
nearest edge
↓
HTML bytes
```

Cloudflare confirms static assets are globally cached and served without Worker invocation by default when an asset matches. ([Cloudflare Docs][4])

That's difficult to beat architecturally.

---

# 25. Why it becomes lightning fast for agents

Even more interestingly, agent speed isn't primarily compute latency.

You're reducing:

```text
round trips
tool decisions
retrieval uncertainty
context duplication
token count
LLM reasoning needed to assemble state
```

Instead of:

```text
agent
→ search
→ inspect 10 results
→ fetch graph
→ fetch evidence
→ fetch reviews
→ reconstruct dependencies
```

you give:

```text
context(ARG019, 8000)
```

and return a **compiled epistemic cache line**.

That's what I would actually call these internally:

> **agent cache lines**

Precompiled bundles optimized for model consumption.

That could become one of Pāṭala's best pieces of infrastructure.

