# 27. The final formal Pāṭala model

I would now make these three planes explicit in every architecture document:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                TRUTH PLANE                   │
│                                              │
│ objects / evidence / events / authority      │
│ PostgreSQL + Pāṭala Kernel                   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                      │
                      ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│               COMPILE PLANE                  │
│                                              │
│ dependencies / hashes / staleness / tasks    │
│ Python + Hermes + DuckDB + optional Rust     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                      │
                      ▼
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 READ PLANE                   │
│                                              │
│ immutable HTML / JSON / bundles / Parquet    │
│ R2 + Cloudflare CDN + thin Workers/MCP       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

And one governing law for each:

```text
TRUTH PLANE
Nothing becomes true because an agent says so.

COMPILE PLANE
Nothing recomputes unless its dependencies changed.

READ PLANE
Nothing computes at request time if bytes could already exist.
```

That is the architecture.

Everything we've found in `fuck-off`—staleness propagation, deterministic reducers, compiled bundles, content addressing, incremental projection DAGs, KG2Code, agent context budgets—slots naturally into those three planes instead of becoming another collection of disconnected technology experiments.

And I think that is sufficiently coherent now that **I would stop researching basic infrastructure alternatives and start building toward this exact target inside Pāṭala**. The remaining research should be narrow benchmarking of individual replaceable kernels, not further redesign of the system.

